# A STAND-IN FOR THE BUILD-MACHINE WRAPPER THAT IS NEVER RUN — register items 467 and 1010.
#
# `a_lane_bound_that_cannot_be_taken_names_its_own_cause` drives the bound READERS of
# `.githooks/rust-gates.sh`, not the lanes themselves. Those readers ask one question about the
# wrapper — `rust_gates_wrapper_present`, which is `[ -n "$BX" ] && [ -x "$BX" ]` — and the answer
# decides which sentence they print. So the fixture needs a path that EXISTS and is EXECUTABLE, and
# it needs nothing else at all.
#
# ⚠⚠ IT IS A TRACKED FILE RATHER THAN ONE THE TEST WRITES, and that is item 467 rather than style:
# Rust's harness runs cases on threads of one process, so a case that writes its own stand-in holds
# a write handle every sibling inherits until its own `exec` — measured at 10 failures in 30 runs
# before that class was fixed. A file the repository carries has no such window.
#
# ⛔ REACHING THIS CODE IS A STAGING FAILURE, NOT A PRODUCT ONE. The readers under test hand the
# wrapper nothing; if this ever runs, a fixture is driving a lane rather than its bound reader, and
# the exit code below is how that says so instead of passing quietly.
echo "lane-bound/bx: this double exists to be TESTED for, never run (register item 1010). Argv: $*" >&2
exit 97
